  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 132 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nRain chances above medium chance (35% to 45%) taper off from west\nto east by Wednesday afternoon with drier weather thereafter and \nwetting rains of at least 1\" to 2\" for locations for highest \nrainfall totals (medium confidence), but an extended period of \nstrong winds is expected to persist through the end of the week. \nThis will be courtesy of the mid to upper storm system lingering \nover the TX PH and CO Front Range through Thursday before losing \nstrength Friday, continuously imparting strong west/southwest \nwinds in the mid to upper troposphere that mix down to the \nsurface. Strongest winds up to High Wind Watch criteria are \ncurrently indicated over the Guadalupes and Delaware Mountains, \nwith gusts above 25 mph elsewhere. A similar story Thursday but \nwith lighter winds is expected as the mid to upper storm system \nbegins to weaken. Each day, afternoon showers/storms also remain \npossible from heating of elevated terrain. This will stay true \neven as highs in the 80s F, mid 70s F higher elevations, and 90s F\nRio Grande basin and Terrell County Wednesday decrease into the \nlower to mid 80s F, upper 60s to mid 70s F higher elevations for \nThursday as a drier, cooler, and more stable air mass attempts to \nmove in behind the storm system. Lows Wednesday night currently \nshow a more refreshing air mass characterized by lows falling \nbelow 65F for most of the area, and then below 60 F for most of \nthe area by Thursday night. While winds stay gusty, winds shifting\nto west/northwest and then northerly by Thursday will allow for \nthe aforementioned cooler and more stable air to finally move into\nthe area. \n\nNortherly/northeasterly winds at the end of the week then prevent\nhighs from rising above the mid 80s F areawide through early \nweekend, largely settling in the 60s and 70s F, with lows below \n60F for all but the Rio Grande basin and Terrell County. \nAfternoon/evening showers/storms will still be possible each \nafternoon/evening across higher elevations (especially Lower Trans\nPecos), but chances should remain low (less than 35%). A warming \ntrend late weekend through early next week is then expected to set\nin as winds return to more east/southeasterly and mid to upper \nridging redevelops over the Desert SW into west Texas.",
